  "query_preview": "..\nTuesday through Friday: Upper-level ridging builds across the region \non Tuesday, initiating a modest warming trend. A weak shortwave \ntemporarily flattens the ridge on Wednesday, bringing increased \ncloud cover and a slight (~15%) chance of showers along the Cascade \ncrest. While some models suggest precipitation may reach the surface \nacross the Okanogan Highlands, but very dry mid-levels and weak \ndynamics leads to very low confidence in these solutions. \n\nThe ridge amplifies Thursday into Friday as a surface thermal trough \nextends northward west of the Cascades, establishing light easterly \nto offshore flow. High temperatures will climb through the end of \nthe work week, peaking in the mid 60s to lower 70s by Friday, which \nis roughly 10 to 15 degrees above seasonal normals. Overnight \ntemperatures will remain chilly before moderating late in the week. \nLows Wednesday morning will drop into the upper 20s to lower 30s \nacross sheltered northern valleys and into the lower to mid 30s \nelsewhere, with milder conditions in the lower 40s near the \nWenatchee area.\n\nSaturday through Monday: Ensembles have come into better agreement \nfor the next weather system moving into the Pacific Northwest \nSaturday. This is rather weak with the wind forecast trending down \nover the last couple of days and the best precipitation chances \nremain at and west of the Cascade crest. \n\nThis opens the door, however, for a deeper trough to move into the \nPacific Northwest early next week. While ensembles generally favor \nthis deeper trough moving into the western US, there remains some \nnotable differences. 71% of ensemble members depict the trough \nshifting inland, delivering cooler temperatures and widespread \nprecipitation chances to the Inland Northwest. Another 21% show the \ntrough digging offshore along the California coast, which would \nallow ridging to continue and keep warmer, drier conditions in place \nlonger. The remaining 8% suggest a weaker, progressive wave with \nminimal impact. Latest National Blend of Models (NBM) guidance \nreflects the dominant scenario with 24-hour precipitation chances \nexceeding 0.10 inches between 5 PM Monday and 5 PM Tuesday ranging \nfrom 40-60% over the mountains, 30-40% across eastern Washington and \nIdaho Panhandle valleys, and 15-25% across central Washington.\n/vmt",
